// Input-parser fixture: a template with two anonymous captures is a tuple, and
// the collection that holds it says so (ADR-092, REP-54).
//
// This is §7.8's own type-derivation row — ``lines(`{int},{int}`)`` is
// `Vec[(Int, Int)]` — which no corpus program covered, which is how the defect
// survived thirty-three of them. The static type was already right; the *Vec's
// element tag* was `Unit`, so `vec_format` rendered every element through
// `unit_format`, which prints the literal string "Unit" and ignores its
// payload.
//
// OBSERVED RED at 6e74642 (the fix removed): line 1 printed `[Unit, Unit]` and
// line 2 printed `false`.
//
// **The `pairs == same` line is the load-bearing half.** Rendering is the
// visible symptom; the wrong `Bool` is the silent one. `vec_equals` compares
// the two element descriptors by pointer and bails before it compares a single
// element, so a parsed `Vec[(Int, Int)]` compared unequal to an identical one
// built with `push` — and its `false` sibling also answered `false`, so both
// spellings agreed with each other and with nothing. Comparing a parser-built
// Vec against a codegen-built one is the point: it gates ADR-042's two
// descriptor producers answering the same tag for the same type.
fn main() -> Int {
    var pairs = read lines(`{int},{int}`);
    out(pairs);
    var same = Vec();
    same.push((1, 2));
    same.push((3, 4));
    out(pairs == same);
    pairs.len()
}
